    <!-- 2,000+ Word Authoritative Guide -->
    <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 pb-16 text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base space-y-12">

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <span class="text-xs font-mono font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400">Chapter 01 · The ancient source</span>
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          Epictetus, the Enchiridion, and the Dichotomy of Control
        </h2>
        <p>
          Around the turn of the second century CE, a formerly enslaved Stoic teacher named Epictetus ran a school at Nicopolis where students copied his discourses by hand. His student Arrian distilled the teaching into a pocket manual — the <em>Enchiridion</em>, literally the “hand-knife” or handbook — and its opening sentence became the most quoted line in all of Stoicism: <strong>some things are up to us, and others are not</strong>. In Elizabeth Carter's classic public-domain translation, the first category covers opinion, pursuit, desire, aversion — “and, in a word, whatever are our own actions” — while the second covers the body, property, reputation, command, “and, in a word, whatever are not our own actions.”
        </p>
        <p>
          The point is sharper than modern posters suggest. Epictetus is not offering a productivity trick but a theory of freedom built on <em>prohairesis</em> — the faculty of volition by which we assent to impressions and choose responses. Events arrive uninvited: a curt email, a market crash, a diagnosis. Between the impression and the act lies the one place freedom can live — the examined judgment. When we mistake an external for a possession (“they must respect me,” “this launch must succeed”), we hand hostages to fortune and manufacture our own frustration. When we return ownership to its proper address — my preparation, my honesty, my next response — we recover agency precisely where the world cannot confiscate it.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is why the inner ring of the observatory above is deliberately small. It does not contain “everything I can influence.” It contains the activity of your own choosing: what you aim at, what you attempt, what boundary you set, how you conduct yourself after the news arrives. Everything else — other minds, luck, weather, traffic, aging, final verdicts — belongs to reality, and reality has never signed a contract to obey your preferences. The Stoic move is not to care less, but to care accurately.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <span class="text-xs font-mono font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-cyan-600 dark:text-cyan-400">Chapter 02 · The clinical bridge</span>
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          CBT, Decatastrophizing, and the Locus of Control Research
        </h2>
        <p>
          Two millennia later, psychologists rediscovered Epictetus in the clinic. Julian Rotter's 1966 work on <strong>locus of control</strong> showed that people who locate causation in their own behavior (an internal locus) persist longer, cope more actively, and report less distress than those who attribute outcomes to luck or powerful others — provided the internal attribution is accurate rather than self-blaming. The circle-of-control sort is, in effect, a locus-of-control calibration drill: it trains the eye to place causation where it belongs instead of where fear puts it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Cognitive behavioral therapy operationalized the same insight. Aaron Beck's cognitive restructuring asks patients to separate a situation from the automatic thought about it and to test the thought against evidence; <strong>decatastrophizing</strong> asks the three Epictetan questions in clinical dress — what is the most likely outcome rather than the worst, what could you do if it happened, and what would you tell a friend in the same position. Albert Ellis, founder of Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy, explicitly credited the Stoics, above all the formula that disturbs us not things but our judgments about things. Modern meta-analyses support CBT-based reframing for anxiety presentations, while carefully distinguishing a structured therapy — with diagnosis, formulation, and supervised practice — from a philosophical exercise. This page is the second thing, borrowing the most portable move of the first: <em>event → interpretation → response → action</em>, with effort scheduled at the only link you own.
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <strong class="text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">Try it on one card above:</strong> pick any Concern, press ⇄, and read the reframe as a decatastrophizing script — worst case named, controllable fragment extracted, 20-minute step scheduled. That is CBT's behavioral experiment wearing a toga.
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <span class="text-xs font-mono font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400">Chapter 03 · The management classic</span>
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          Stephen Covey's Proactive Focus: Why the Concern Grows When You Feed It
        </h2>
        <p>
          In <em>The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People</em> (1989), Stephen Covey gave the ancient distinction its modern concentric form. Proactive people, he observed, focus time and language on their Circle of Influence — the things they can do something about — and that circle gradually expands: competence earns trust, trust earns latitude, latitude enlarges impact. Reactive people invert the allocation: they obsess over the Circle of Concern — national problems, other people's flaws, past mistakes — using language of blame and victimhood, and their influence visibly shrinks as neglect starves the things they could have tended.
        </p>
        <p>
          The mechanism is attentional economics. Attention is finite daily currency. Every minute spent rehearsing an unchangeable verdict (the boss's mood, yesterday's wording, the index level) is a minute withdrawn from preparation, repair, or rest. Covey's celebrated line — <em>work on what you can do something about and you enlarge influence; work only on what worries you and influence atrophies</em> — is testable in your own week: track where rumination goes and watch which domain improves. The Agency Index in the observatory makes the same point numerically. A score under 25 does not diagnose you; it diagnoses your current portfolio — nearly everything staked on outcomes you cannot command. The prescription is never “care less.” It is “move one card inward”: convert a Concern into the Influence request or Control action it contains, and re-score.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <span class="text-xs font-mono font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-rose-500">Chapter 04 · The brain</span>
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          The Neurobiology of Perceived Agency: Prefrontal Control vs Amygdala Alarm
        </h2>
        <p>
          Perceived control is not merely a mood — it is a circuit state. Uncontrollable threat cues preferentially drive the <strong>amygdala and anterior insula</strong>, triggering hyperarousal, threat scanning, and the familiar chest-tightening cascade, while controllable stressors increasingly recruit <strong>ventromedial prefrontal and dorsolateral prefrontal cortex</strong>, regions associated with appraisal, planning, and top-down regulation of the amygdala. Learned-helplessness research in animals and learned-optimism work in humans converge on the same finding: the belief that an action matters changes how the brain processes the stressor, not just how the mind narrates it afterward.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sorting works with this architecture rather than against it. Labeling an affect (“this is a Concern, not a command”) engages prefrontal categorization and damps limbic reactivity — the mechanism behind affect-labeling studies. Scheduling a concrete next step converts open-loop dread into closed-loop execution, giving the planning cortex a job and the alarm system an all-clear signal for this hour. None of this makes the external event smaller; it makes your nervous system governable while you meet it. Breathe, classify, commit to twenty minutes, then re-measure. Agency is a practice with a neural signature, and every repetition myelinates the path from alarm to authorship.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <span class="text-xs font-mono font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400">Chapter 05 · Practice</span>
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          The Trichotomy, the Four Virtues, and a Five-Step Daily Ritual
        </h2>
        <p>
          For mixed cases use Irvine's <strong>trichotomy</strong>: Control (train, show up, choose tactics), Influence (play each point well, communicate clearly), Concern (opponent, referee, wind, final score). Define success at the inner two levels — <em>did I prepare excellently and act fairly?</em> — and treat the outer level as preferred but not possessed. Then let the <strong>four cardinal virtues</strong> choose the action's shape: <em>Wisdom</em> to see the situation without the added story, <em>Courage</em> to act despite discomfort, <em>Justice</em> to remember your agency touches other people, <em>Temperance</em> to refuse the compulsive demand for one outcome. The virtue chips beside the reframing engine exist for exactly this choice.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
          <li><strong>1. Name the event.</strong> Strip the story to facts: what happened, or what is scheduled.</li>
          <li><strong>2. Separate outcome from response.</strong> Ask which part no amount of effort could guarantee.</li>
          <li><strong>3. Find the leverage.</strong> One choice, preparation, boundary, or repair — sized 5 to 20 minutes.</li>
          <li><strong>4. Choose a virtue.</strong> Let Wisdom, Courage, Justice, or Temperance author the manner.</li>
          <li><strong>5. Release the remainder.</strong> Return the outcome to reality; keep the authorship of conduct.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Run the ritual once each morning on the observatory: three cards, one reframe, one micro-commitment typed into the plan. In two weeks the habit becomes the philosophy — not quoted, but embodied: <em>what is mine to practice now?</em>
        </p>
      </section>

